# wrproj lifestyle tracker

Project: `C:\Users\aware\OneDrive\Desktop\wrproj` (not a git repo)

## Critical environment gotchas

- **Never use bare `python`.** It resolves to a 3.14 install with none of the
  packages; it starts fine then dies with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named
  'cv2'`, which looks like a missing package but is the wrong interpreter.
  Use **`py -3.12`** (= `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Python\Python312\python.exe`,
  has cv2 4.13.0 / ultralytics / psutil / numpy).
- `.ps1` files are blocked — CurrentUser execution policy is Undefined, so it
  falls back to Restricted. Either run `py -3.12 src/....py` directly, or the
  user sets `Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned` (his call).
- `run.ps1` is a launcher that finds a working interpreter by *importing cv2*,
  not by checking PATH. `.\run.ps1 doctor | log | today | validate | analyze`.
- torch here is the **CPU-only** build. No GPU inference. (Ollama *does* use
  the 4060 — that is a separate runtime, not torch.)
- **The console is cp1252**, and `profile.json` is deliberately full of emoji.
  Printing a block name dies with `UnicodeEncodeError` in the *reporting* path,
  which is a miserable way to lose a working run. Every script with a `__main__`
  that prints profile text calls `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8",
  errors="replace")` first.

## What this is

A whole-life activity tracker, NOT the original productivity punisher. It
writes a timeline of what he was doing all day. Three inputs, none sufficient
alone:

- **camera** → WHERE he is + HOW he's sitting (desk / bed / gone; posture)
- **screen** → WHAT is in front of him (VS Code, Canvas, Steam, Crunchyroll)
- **clock** → WHETHER THAT'S FINE (bed + anime at 23:30 is `recovery`; the
  identical camera frame and window title at 14:00 is `watching_in_bed`)

Both sensors report **facts, not judgments** — e.g. the screen sensor keeps
`category` and `idle` separate, because "no input for 5 min" means opposite
things for a playing video vs an open editor, and only the camera can tell
those apart. Ground truth for "on plan" is **The Docket's plan**.

## Built and tested

Every file above has a module-level docstring stating its own role — `ls src/`
plus a quick read gets you the same map this table used to give.

Logs are one JSONL line per **run** (contiguous stretch in a mode), not per
sample, in `logs/modes-YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl`. Debounce (`min_run_seconds: 90`)
stops an 8-second Discord glance from splitting a work block; committed runs
are **backdated to when the mode actually began**, not when it was confirmed
(tested — runs must be contiguous with no gap).

## Measured facts — do not re-derive

### Detectors

- Pose is **both faster and better** than Haar: ~32ms vs ~75ms per frame at
  720p. Haar on 4K was 523ms with *lower* confidence, hence the downscale to
  `MAX_WIDTH = 1280` in `analyze_session.py`.
- Person/bed **box overlap was 0.25 while sitting at the desk** — no threshold
  can separate bed from desk that way. Torso angle (shoulder→hip vector)
  replaced it. `UPRIGHT_MAX=35°`, `RECLINED_MAX=65°`.
- A frontal Haar cascade matched a **photo collage on the wall**, scoring an
  empty room as productive. Faces are searched with the *profile* cascade and
  only *inside* YOLO's person box.
- Burst voting thresholds are deliberately asymmetric: presence takes a plain
  majority (YOLO is stable both ways), head_up needs only 1-in-3 (the cascade
  has high precision, poor recall — a hit is strong evidence, a miss is weak).
- Window-title matching: browser needles match the **page title, not the URL**.
  `notion.so` never appears in a window title; `Command Center - Notion` does.

### Scored against ground truth (2026-08-12)

IMG_9874 (24:21), labelled blind by Claude from contact sheets, 287 scorable
bursts — `sessions/IMG_9874_analysis/labels.json` + `score.json`:

| detector | correct | remaining errors |
|---|---|---|
| haar | **66%** | 88 at_desk→`present_unclear`, 9 at_desk→`absent` |
| pose | **91%** | 24 at_desk→`reclined`, 1 at_desk→`lying_down` |

**That session contains exactly one true class.** It proves the detector does
not hallucinate absence during desk work; it validates *nothing* about bed,
absence, or phone. Do not quote 91% as general accuracy.

### Three bugs, one pattern

All three are **a sensor failure wearing a state's name** — the exact thing the
facts-not-judgments rule exists to prevent. Expect more of these; the tell is a
`Counter((output, upstream_flag))` that collapses to a diagonal.

0. **`posture` never reached `place()` in production.** FIXED 2026-08-12.
   Posture was voted in `analyze_session.reduce_burst()` and nowhere else,
   while `CameraState.reduce()` — the only reducer the *live* path used —
   never returned a `posture` key at all. `pose_detector.place()` reads
   `state.get("posture")`, so it was permanently `"unknown"`: **`reclined` and
   `lying_down` were unreachable in `mode_log.py` and the dashboard**, and the
   whole bed-at-night branch of `resolve_mode()` was dead at runtime while
   looking alive in the code *and* in the scored replays. Two reducers meant
   the thing being scored was not the thing being run. The logic now lives in
   `CameraState.reduce` and `reduce_burst` is a thin alias; verified
   behaviour-identical on 4000/4000 randomised bursts.

1. **`present_unclear` ⟺ `head_up == False`, exactly.** 1:1 across all 293
   bursts, zero exceptions — it carries no independent information, it is a
   rename of "the profile cascade missed." 93/293 = 32% of that session, and
   pose calls 74 of those 93 (80%) `at_desk`. Fixed by using pose.

2. **A dead Iriun feed logs as `away`.** FIXED 2026-08-12. When the phone drops
   off WiFi, Iriun does *not* fail the read — it streams a placeholder card (a
   cartoon cat on black, "Looking for the phone"). So `grab()` returns a valid
   frame, `frame is None` never fires, **`reconnect()` never runs**, YOLO
   truthfully reports no person → `place()`=`absent` → `resolve_mode()`=`away`.
   An hour of dropout reads as an hour of him being out, and nothing in the log
   distinguishes it. Now detected in `capture.py` (`Camera.stalled`) and
   surfaced as its own `camera_lost` state that **must never collapse into
   `absent`** — `absent` is a claim about *him*, `camera_lost` about *the rig*.
   The screen sensor still gets a say, since typing proves a human regardless
   of the camera.
   - A real sensor cannot **emit** two byte-identical frames. Consecutive
     *exposures* of a motionless scene differ by ~0.5 mean absolute value
     (shot/thermal noise, measured on this rig).
   - **That is NOT the same as two identical READS, and the original
     "needs no threshold" claim here was wrong.** `grab()` compares reads, and
     the tracker loop polls every ~30ms to keep the MJPEG stream fed. Measured
     2026-08-17 at the 4K the capture now requests: Iriun delivers **4.6 fps**,
     so **68% of reads are byte-identical**, longest run 7, and `STALL_FRAMES=3`
     tripped **1.7 seconds** into a working session. Ari saw `camera_lost`
     stamped over a live feed of himself. Raising the resolution to 4K — a
     documented improvement elsewhere in this file — is what broke it, by
     cutting delivered fps far below the poll rate.
   - Fixed by adding `STALL_SECONDS = 3.0`: the streak must hold **and** the
     image must have been unchanged that long. Wall-clock is the
     poll-rate-independent form of the same question, so it survives future
     resolution/fps/burst changes. `Camera.clock` is injectable purely so
     `test_stall.py` can move time; test `[1b]` reproduces the false positive
     at the measured numbers and asserts it stays quiet.
   - Burst voting makes it *worse*, not better — 5 votes on 5 copies of one
     frame return the single-frame answer at 5× apparent confidence.

3. **`posture_from_angle()` discarded lean direction.** FIXED 2026-08-12, but
   not the way it first looked. `torso_angle()` has a whole docstring on why
   the sign must be preserved; `posture_from_angle()` threw it away one
   function later with `dev = abs(angle - upright)`, so leaning *forward* over
   the keyboard and leaning *back* produced the same number. All 25 pose errors
   deviated in the **same direction**: median −6.1° against the −47.0°
   baseline = +40.9°, consistently positive, never negative.
   - Ruled out first: it is *not* the `_mid()` single-keypoint fallback. All 25
     misclassified frames had **both** hips visible at `KP_CONF`. Don't re-test.
   - The real fix was to delete the middle class rather than sharpen it — see
     "Posture is self-calibrating" below. `reclined` was what demanded a
     precise baseline *and* what routed a desk-chair lean to
     `watching_in_bed`. With two classes ~90° apart the sign stops mattering.

### Posture is self-calibrating — do not add a calibration step back

`src/torso_baseline.py`. **There is no `UPRIGHT_TORSO_ANGLE` to set any more**,
and `--calibrate` is superseded for the live path.

Ari's own argument, and it is correct: the camera is a phone he picks up for
unrelated reasons several times a day, so an absolute baseline is stale within
hours and there is no moment at which it is reliably right. Worse, it fails
*silently* — every posture reading is simply wrong and nothing says so.

So the system stores a habit instead of an angle: he is upright the
overwhelming majority of the time he is in frame (IMG_9874: 100% at_desk over
24 minutes), therefore **the running median of observed torso angles IS his
upright posture**. Move the camera and the median follows within about an hour
of use. Verified in simulation: baseline relearned −35.6 → 11.9 after a +50°
shift with no intervention.

- **Median, not mean** — a genuine lying-down stretch must not drag the
  baseline; the median of a mostly-upright window ignores the tail.
- **Every angle is fed in, including ones judged `lying`.** Filtering to
  "frames that already look upright" would make the baseline confirm whatever
  it currently believes — the circularity that lets a wrong baseline persist.
- **Two classes, 55° gate.** `reclined` is deleted. It was the class that
  demanded precision (a narrow band in the middle) *and* the one that logged a
  desk-chair lean as `watching_in_bed`. Upright vs horizontal is ~90° apart,
  so 55° cannot be confused by drift. `UPRIGHT_MAX`/`RECLINED_MAX` still exist
  as aliases of `LYING_MIN` so old references don't break.
- Under 12 samples it reports `unknown`, never a shaky guess.
- Read it with `py -3.12 src/torso_baseline.py` or `GET /api/baseline`.

### The rig itself

- **Resolution has to be ASKED FOR, and for weeks nothing did.** `cv2.Video
  Capture` takes the driver default (640×480 here) no matter what the device
  supports; `capture.py` never called `CAP_PROP_FRAME_WIDTH/HEIGHT`. The rig
  was always capable of 4K at 30fps on both MSMF and DSHOW. Now requested via
  `DEFAULT_WIDTH/HEIGHT`. (Supersedes an earlier note in this file claiming the
  live feed "is 640×480" — it was, but only by omission.)
- **Iriun pads every 16:9 mode**: the real image is exactly 75% of the
  requested width *and* height, centred, black on all four sides — so 43% of a
  4K frame is nothing. 4:3 modes are not padded. Measured across the ladder:

  | asked | got | real content | used |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | 3840×2160 | 3840×2160 | 2880×1624 | 56.4% |
  | 2560×1440 | 2560×1440 | 1920×1082 | 56.4% |
  | 1920×1080 | 1920×1080 | 1440×812 | 56.4% |
  | 1600×900 | 1280×960 | 1280×722 | 75.2% |
  | 640×480 | 640×480 | 640×360 | 75.0% |

  4K still carries the most real detail, so `capture.py` keeps 4K and
  **auto-crops** the border (`AUTOCROP`), giving a clean 2880×1624. That turns
  the 1280px detection input from 960px of content into a full 1280 — a free
  1.33×. The box is detected from the per-pixel *max* across several frames,
  never one: a dark corner of the room reads as padding in a single exposure,
  but real pixels carry sensor noise and padding is identically black. It
  refuses to crop unless the result is a clean border ≥35% of the frame,
  because cropping away part of the room is worse than leaving it black.
- **Pose at 4K costs only 3.5 ms more than at 1280** (24.2 vs 20.7), because
  YOLO resizes to 640×640 internally and both paths converge before inference.
  The 523 ms 4K figure elsewhere in this file is **Haar**, which ran on the
  full-resolution person crop. `MAX_WIDTH` was never about YOLO.
- Detection is pinned to 1280 anyway (`camera_state.DETECT_WIDTH`), matching
  `analyze_session.py`'s `MAX_WIDTH`, so the 91% score describes what is
  actually running. This is about comparability, not speed — and it lives in
  `CameraState.raw_burst` so every consumer inherits it rather than each
  caller remembering.
- Streaming is where 4K genuinely hurts: **462 KB/frame at q78 vs 97 KB at
  1280** (5.5 MB/s vs 1.2 at 12fps), plus a 4× heavier decode in the browser.
- **Changing resolution changes the FRAMING, not just the pixel count** —
  640×480 is 4:3 and 4K is 16:9, a different sensor crop. This used to
  invalidate `UPRIGHT_TORSO_ANGLE`; the self-calibrating baseline now absorbs
  it automatically, which is most of why that design was worth it.
- `src/test_stall.py`'s `camera_with()` builds a `Camera` via `__new__`, so
  every new attribute `grab()` touches must be added there too. It is meant to
  break — it caught `crop`.
- **The camera angle changes drastically between sessions.** IMG_9874 is a high
  overhead shot looking down at the desk (~45° tilt); the live 2026-08-12 rig
  was a low wide room shot. `UPRIGHT_TORSO_ANGLE` is per-mount and must be
  recalibrated whenever the phone is remounted — `analyze_session.py --calibrate`.
- The **bed sits directly behind him in frame**, which is the geometric reason
  the person/bed box overlap could never have worked.
- IMG_9874 is badly **overexposed** (large window behind the desk) — the
  opposite of the unsolved purple-night problem.

### Claude-as-detector economics

- A frame read in chat costs ~1.1k tokens at 960px. At the production 15s
  interval that is ~260k tokens/hour of pure image before any reasoning, so
  **frame-at-a-time judging is permanently off the table**, not just expensive.
  `src/claude_judge.py` (one frame → one word, over the API) is the old shape.
- What *is* cheap is breadth: a contact sheet puts 30–49 timestamps in one read
  for roughly the price of one frame. So Claude never runs the timeline — it
  **labels** it, and the labels become permanent ground truth.
- Sheet density measured: 8×8 at 200×150 is readable for presence/place but
  marginal for posture. `claude_eyes.py` defaults to 6×5 at 320×240.
- **Ground-truth sheets must be blind.** A tile pre-printed with `at_desk`
  anchors the judgement, and truth contaminated by the thing it scores is
  worthless. The colour bar has to go too — the detector's answer is readable
  off a sheet without reading a word. `--audit` shows the guess, and is the
  right mode for hunting *where* it's wrong.
- Claude's label vocabulary deliberately excludes `present_unclear`. When the
  image genuinely cannot settle it that is `unsure`, a different claim.

## PSYCHOWL — the federation (2026-08-12)

The tracker process IS the psychowl server. One process, one origin, because
(a) the camera has exactly one owner, and (b) DATA-CONTRACT.md proves the
browser-side alternative impossible — localStorage is per-origin, Google iCal
has no CORS header. Federation, not a rewrite: every sibling project keeps
working standalone; `src/psychowl_data.py` is the read layer (disk only,
absence-tolerant). `src/dashboard.py`'s `do_GET` is the route reference now —
every route is commented there (`/`, `/tracker`, `/focus`, `/grimoire/`,
`/ics?cal=n`, `/brief`, `/solo`, `/api/psychowl`); the one thing worth
restating here is that `/focus` serves FocusFlow **v1**
(`FocusFlow-and-Art/focusflow.html`), NOT v2 — that was his explicit call, not
an accident, and it's the one route decision the code comment alone wouldn't
flag as deliberate.

The old NightOwl static hub is **retired**: `no hub` now probes
`/api/psychowl`, starts the server headless via pythonw if down (resolved from
`py -3.12`'s own executable — the `pyw` PATH shim no-ops under Start-Process),
and opens the URL. `no sync` still rebuilds the legacy `hub/index.html`;
harmless, unused. Aria stays her own server — psychowl shows her line from
`/api/state`, never absorbs her. Solo Leveling's EXP bridge still reads the
retired `activity_log.csv` → repointing it at `logs/modes-*.jsonl` is OPEN.

## The dashboard (`py -3.12 src/dashboard.py` → 127.0.0.1:8787)

Left column is live MJPEG on top, a strip of moments underneath; right column
is the deviation from Perfect Ari right now, plus Aria's line. All local.

- **Python owns the camera, the browser does not.** A webcam has one owner, so
  `getUserMedia` on the same device would fight the detector. The page gets
  MJPEG from this process instead, which also guarantees the pane shows the
  exact frame that was judged.
- **Stored frames are a deliberate, narrow exception** to the project's
  no-frames-stored promise: one thumbnail per *mode change*, capped at 40,
  oldest deleted, `web/moments/`, wiped by `--reset`.
- **`absent` is often the plan working.** Roughly half of Perfect Ari's day is
  away from the desk (gym, dance, shower, lunch, meal prep), so `profile.json`
  carries `away_ok` per block. Without it the panel would flag him hardest at
  the moments he was most on plan.
- Blocks tile the day half-open and wrap past midnight, so 01:00 Night ritual
  covers 01:00–07:00 and sleeping never reads as drift.
- Aria is rate-limited to one turn per 120s, so her line can lag the verdict.
  The UI marks a stale line with the status she was actually reacting to —
  unmarked, "back on track!" under a DRIFTING banner reads as her being wrong.

### Which model is allowed to claim what

| | sees | may assert |
|---|---|---|
| pose detector | frames | place + posture (91% scored) |
| window sensor | foreground window | app, category, idle |
| Aria (qwen2.5:7b) | **a facts table, never an image** | one line about the gap |
| moondream | the frame | tile caption only, marked unverified, **OFF** |

Aria runs through her own server (`aria-assistant/run_server.py`, port 8000) on
session id `lifestyle-tracker` so tracker pings never pollute the conversation
he actually has with her. `server.py`'s own design note is "one brain, many
thin clients" — this is another thin client, not a second personality. Her
`modes` (study/gym/dance/gamer) already answer "how hard should this push":
study mode says the bit gets one line at most because every joke costs him
re-entry. Do NOT auto-switch her mode — `assistant/modes.py` is explicit that
an inferred mode is worse than none. Her reply returns the emotion in a `tag`
field; the engine has already stripped it from the text.

If her server is down the page degrades to facts-only. It must never
substitute a generic message — a line attributed to her that she did not say
is worse than no line.

### moondream: measured, and switched off

`VISION_CAPTIONS = False` in `dashboard.py` is a result, not an unfinished
feature. On 2026-08-12, given a live frame where he was **standing across the
room with an empty chair**, moondream answered:

> "a person sitting in an office chair... working on their computer...
> requires focus and concentration."

It inverted the single most important fact and then invented a mental state.
Other findings from the same session: yes/no questions return empty strings
entirely; open captions are object lists that never mention the person
("urns on a wooden table, black mouse and keyboard..."); and it appends
unfalsifiable mood claims ("likely taking a break or relaxing") unprompted.
`num_predict: 40` was also truncating those object lists into nonsense.
It does genuinely see images — it described a solid red square correctly — the
failure is action recognition, not plumbing. The pose detector scores 91% on
exactly this question. Re-test with llava:7b before turning captions on.

Turning a new recording into a scored regression test is now the
`label-footage` skill (`.claude/skills/label-footage/`), not inline here —
it's a task-specific procedure, not something every session needs loaded.

## Open items

1. **A ~90 min class recording is still needed** — desk work, breaks, walking
   around, and lying in bed replicating anime-watching. Sharper reason now:
   IMG_9874 is 100% `at_desk`, so it can score exactly one class. Nothing
   validates `lying_down` / `absent` / `on_phone` yet.
2. **Purple night lighting is an unsolved problem.** His room lights are mainly
   purple at night. `cv2.BGR2GRAY` is BT.601 (`0.299R + 0.587G + 0.114B`) —
   green carries 59% of luminance and purple LEDs starve it, so the Haar
   cascade's input loses most of its contrast while the frame still looks
   bright. Pose (CNN, takes BGR) should degrade far more gracefully. Worst
   part: the only mode that occurs under purple light is `recovery`. Needs
   **night footage**, then compare alternative grayscale conversions (HSV V
   channel, LAB L, red-channel-only, + CLAHE). `src/lighting.py` NOT built yet.
3. **Phone detection is off** (`trust_phone_detection: false`) because his
   phone IS the camera (Iriun) — phone-in-hand is undetectable by construction,
   so any firing is necessarily a false positive. **Still to do:** `PoseDetector`
   defaults to `phone_model="yolov8n.pt"` and runs a *second* full YOLO per
   frame for it. Pass `phone_model=None` to halve pose inference cost.
4. **`should_intervene()` in `src/mode_log.py` is a deliberate stub** — left for
   him to write, because it's a values decision. Open questions he was given:
   how long is too long, which modes qualify (`work_paused` is risky — that may
   be thinking), night exemption, and a repeat-rate guard so the card doesn't
   fire every 15s. House rule for the card: **every claim in `-Message` must
   come from recorded data**; one invented observation and it reads as a gimmick
   forever.
5. **Recurring footage loop.** PARTLY DONE — `claude_eyes.py` closes the label
   half: IMG_9874 now has `labels.json` + `score.json` and is a live regression
   test. Remaining: run it on each new video as it arrives, and keep the `.MOV`
   in `sessions/` (IMG_9874 was originally left in `Downloads/`, so the sheets
   could not be rebuilt from it).
6. **`arbitrate()` in `src/claude_eyes.py` is a stub** — decides whether
   Claude's label (better, but stale by minutes) or the local detector's
   (worse, but current) goes in today's log. Local wins until he chooses. Three
   sketched policies are in the docstring; `tie-break` alone would fix most of
   the `present_unclear` damage.
7. ~~Sign fix for `posture_from_angle()`~~ DONE — resolved by deleting the
   `reclined` class and self-calibrating the baseline, not by fixing the sign.
   Do not reintroduce a middle posture class or a manual calibration step.
8. ~~`plan_for_now()` returns `None`~~ DONE — `src/profile_plan.py` answers it
   from `profile.json` (Perfect Ari's day). The Docket is still not wired; when
   it is, it should override the profile rather than replace it. `None` must
   still mean *unknown*, never *off-plan*.
9. Health data integration — format never established.
10. `src/main.py` (has a `NotImplementedError` stub) and `src/reinforcement.py`
    (tkinter screen flash) are from the abandoned punisher design. Superseded by
    `mode_log.py`, not yet removed. `src/claude_judge.py` joins them —
    superseded by `claude_eyes.py`, and its productive/lazy vocabulary is from
    the punisher era.

## Working style

Concise for work. He wants insight into *why* choices are made, not just code.
Prefer measuring over guessing — the whole record-then-replay architecture
exists so detector changes are scored on identical footage instead of vibes.
